---
import CalcPage from '../../components/CalcPage.astro';
import Field from '../../components/Field.astro';
---

<CalcPage
  title="Ratio strength, ppm & ppb"
  kicker="Concentration"
  lede="Parts per million sounds abstract until you notice it is just mg/L. Parts per billion is just mcg/L. Enter a concentration in whatever form the question gave it to you, and this converts it by canceling units."
  description="Convert between ppm, ppb, mg/L, mcg/L, percentage strength and ratio strength using dimensional analysis."
>
  <form slot="form" id="calc">
    <div class="field-grid">
      <div class="field field--wide">
        <label for="basis">Basis</label>
        <select id="basis" name="basis">
          <option value="w/v">w/v — solid dissolved in a liquid</option>
          <option value="w/w">w/w — solid mixed into a solid</option>
          <option value="v/v">v/v — liquid mixed into a liquid</option>
        </select>
      </div>
    </div>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>What the question gave you</legend>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <div class="field">
          <label for="known">Form</label>
          <select id="known" name="known">
            <option value="ratio">Ratio strength, as 1 : X</option>
            <option value="percent">Percentage strength (%)</option>
            <option value="ppm">Parts per million (ppm)</option>
            <option value="ppb">Parts per billion (ppb)</option>
            <option value="conc">Concentration (mg/mL, mg/g)</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <Field
          label="Value"
          name="value"
          placeholder="10000"
          hint="For a ratio strength of 1:10,000 enter just the 10000."
        />
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>How you want the answer</legend>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <div class="field field--wide">
          <label for="report">Report the concentration as</label>
          <select id="report" name="report">
            <option value="ppm">Parts per million — mg/L</option>
            <option value="ppb">Parts per billion — mcg/L</option>
          </select>
          <span class="hint">
            Both are always shown in the table. This just picks which one gets
            the headline and the worked solution.
          </span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <fieldset>
      <legend>Optional — how much are you actually making?</legend>
      <div class="field-grid">
        <Field
          label="Total quantity of preparation"
          name="quantity"
          placeholder="500"
          hint="In mL for w/v and v/v, or in g for w/w. Fill this in to also get the amount you need to weigh out."
          wide
        />
      </div>
    </fieldset>

    <div class="field-actions">
      <button class="btn btn--primary" type="submit">Convert</button>
      <button class="btn btn--quiet" type="reset">Clear</button>
    </div>

    <div class="field-actions" style="margin-top:0.9rem">
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"basis":"w/v","known":"ratio","value":"10000","report":"ppm","quantity":"500"}'
      >
        Example: 1:10,000 epinephrine
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"basis":"w/v","known":"percent","value":"0.9","report":"ppm","quantity":"1000"}'
      >
        Example: 0.9% saline
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"basis":"w/v","known":"ppm","value":"4","report":"ppm","quantity":""}'
      >
        Example: 4 ppm fluoride
      </button>
      <button
        class="btn btn--quiet btn--tiny"
        type="button"
        data-example='{"basis":"w/v","known":"ppb","value":"50","report":"ppb","quantity":""}'
      >
        Example: 50 ppb contaminant
      </button>
    </div>
  </form>

  <div slot="concept">
    <h2>ppm is mg/L. That's the whole trick.</h2>
    <p>
      "One part per million" is hard to picture. But work out what it means for
      a liter of water and it becomes something you can hold in your head:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">1 ppm  =  1 mg/L  =  1 mcg/mL
1 ppb  =  1 mcg/L  =  1 ng/mL</pre>

    <p>
      A liter of water weighs 1000 g, which is a million milligrams. So one
      milligram in that liter is literally one part in a million. The
      abstraction and the practical measurement are the same number, and once
      you see that you can stop converting and just read it off.
    </p>

    <p>
      For solids the same logic gives <strong>1 ppm = 1 mg/kg</strong>, since a
      kilogram is also a million milligrams.
    </p>

    <h3>Getting there by canceling units</h3>
    <p>
      Every conversion on this page is done the same way: start with one liter
      (or one kilogram) of the preparation, multiply by factors that turn it
      into the unit you want, and let the units cancel. For a 1:10,000 w/v
      solution:
    </p>

    <pre class="formula">      1000 mL     1 g       1000 mg
1 L × ─────── × ───────── × ───────  =  100 mg
       1 L      10 000 mL     1 g</pre>

    <p>
      Liters cancel, milliliters cancel, grams cancel, and milligrams are all
      that survive. 100 mg in 1 L is 100 mg/L, which is 100 ppm. No formula to
      remember and nothing to set up upside down.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p>
        <strong>The one assumption.</strong> ppm = mg/L holds because the
        solution is mostly water, and water is 1 g/mL. For dilute aqueous
        preparations that is close enough to exact. For a syrup or a
        concentrated solution with a specific gravity well away from 1, the
        equivalence drifts and you would work from mass rather than volume.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</CalcPage>

<script>
  import { setupCalculator } from '../../scripts/calc.js';
  import { solveRatioStrength } from '../../scripts/solvers/ratio-strength.js';

  setupCalculator({
    form: '#calc',
    solve: solveRatioStrength,
  });
</script>
